[- Concept: WebAssembly target. -]
[page id='concept-wasm'
  [meta
    title='WebAssembly Target'
    layout='concept'
    nav-order='260'
    audience='evaluator,user'
    summary='WASM is the long-term distribution target. C ABI is the current mechanism. The boundary moves, the contract stays.'
  ]

  [section id='why' title='Why WebAssembly'
    [prose CX ships today as a native C ABI library — libcx.dylib on macOS, libcx.so on Linux — that every binding loads through its own foreign-function machinery (ctypes for Python, cgo for Go, koffi for TypeScript, P/Invoke for C#, JNA for Java/Kotlin, FFI gem for Ruby, swift-c for Swift, bindgen for Rust). It works and it is fast, but it carries two ongoing costs. Every supported triple needs a binary; every binding carries its own FFI plumbing. A WebAssembly target collapses both.]
    [prose The long-term distribution target is a single libcx.wasm artifact loaded by each binding through its host runtime — wasmtime, wasmer, the browser, Node, V8, Wasmer-Python. The function names, the binary wire protocol, and the per-language Document API all stay the same. The boundary moves; the contract stays.]
  ]

  [section id='today' title='What the C ABI Gives Us Today'
    [list
      [item path='reference/cli'   label='libcx compiled to a platform-specific .dylib or .so']
      [item path='bindings/c-abi'  label='include/cx.h exporting ~49 functions (the full surface)']
      [item path='bindings/index'  label='Per-language FFI binding (ctypes, cgo, extern "C", koffi, P/Invoke, JNA, FFI, swift-c)']
      [item path='install'         label='Platform binaries: macOS arm64 + Linux x86_64 today; per-triple expansion as bindings need them']
    ]
  ]

  [section id='changes' title='What Changes With WebAssembly'
    [prose The shape of the artifact and the loader change. The function surface does not.]
    [list
      [item path='reference/cli'      label='libcx compiles to libcx.wasm — one portable binary, no per-triple build matrix']
      [item path='bindings/c-abi'     label='No per-language FFI setup; each binding loads the WASM module through the host runtime instead']
      [item path='reference/data-bin' label='Memory crosses linear-memory boundaries: caller writes the input string into WASM linear memory, reads the output back out, calls cx_free in the module']
      [item path='reference/grammar'  label='Same function names, same (const char*, char**) → char* contract — projected onto linear-memory offsets']
    ]
  ]

  [section id='stable' title='What Stays the Same'
    [prose The contract is what matters; the loader is implementation.]
    [list
      [item path='bindings/c-abi'      label='Every function name and semantic — the ~49-entry surface in include/cx.h']
      [item path='reference/ast-bin'   label='The ast-bin wire protocol — byte-identical, untouched by the loader change']
      [item path='reference/data-bin'  label='The data-bin format and Arrow C-Data ABI — same bytes, same chunked-column semantics']
      [item path='bindings/index'      label='The Document API in each binding — native data structures over the same wire bytes, unaffected']
      [item path='reference/errors'    label='The error contract per language — same conventions, same code surface']
    ]
  ]

  [section id='binding-deltas' title='Per-Binding Changes When WASM Lands'
    [prose Catch-up is a binding-level swap, not a rewrite. Four steps per binding.]
    [list
      [item path='bindings/index' label='Replace the FFI loader (ctypes / cgo / koffi / P/Invoke / JNA / FFI gem / swift-c) with a WASM runtime loader for that language']
      [item path='bindings/index' label='Replace direct memory pointers with read/write against the WASM linear-memory buffer']
      [item path='bindings/index' label='Keep the binary decoders unchanged — ast-bin and data-bin payloads carry the same bytes']
      [item path='bindings/index' label='Keep the Document API implementation unchanged — it operates on the decoded tree, not on the FFI boundary']
    ]
  ]

  [section id='status' title='Status'
    [prose libcx-wasm is not yet built. The C ABI is the only distribution mechanism on v0.7.0; the WASM target is tracked for the v0.7.x window. A reference WASM build will land alongside a parity-tested rollout across the 10-binding matrix — until that point, bindings MUST link against the C ABI and SHOULD NOT assume the WASM artifact exists. The playgrounds and live-eval scenarios that reference libcx-wasm in their messaging are forward-looking; they fall back to canned outputs today.]
    [prose Track concrete progress in the GitHub repo. When libcx-wasm reaches a usable artifact, the Playground page swaps its lookup-table evaluator for the live module without changing the page UI.]
  ]

